evict-on-save

From: Victor Araújo <victor@victor-software-house.com>

Downstream-Reason: A snapshot taken to free host memory reads every guest page back in and writes it through the host page cache, so under memory pressure the save needs the memory it is meant to free. PUT /snapshot/create takes an optional evict flag: the memory file is written with O_DIRECT (buffered with POSIX_FADV_DONTNEED where the file system refuses O_DIRECT) in 2 MiB chunks, an all-zero chunk of a full snapshot or an all-zero dirty run of a diff merged into its memory file is punched out as a hole, and each chunk is discarded from guest memory once it is written. The microVM is then marked evicted and a resume is refused. The VMM thread's seccomp filters allow the pwrite64 of each chunk, and the fallback's fadvise64 and sync_file_range with only the arguments it passes.
Upstream-Status: not-submitted
Drop-When: Upstream Firecracker writes snapshot memory without the page cache and frees it as it goes.
